# The deployed asset set should be the page, not the project.
#
# This directory is deployed whole, so without something stopping them the docs, the database
# schema and the tests are all fetchable on the hostname. Nothing in them is secret -- they are
# public in this repository -- but they are not the site, and every one of them is a file an
# attacker reads first to learn how the thing is wired.
#
# gitignore syntax. The platform already withholds `_headers`, `_redirects`, `_routes.json`,
# `_worker.js`, `functions/`, `node_modules/`, `.git/` and `.wrangler/` without being asked.
# It does NOT withhold this file, which is why `_redirects` also covers it.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE DOES NOTHING HERE. `_redirects` IS WHAT WORKS. The classic Pages uploader walks
# the directory against the FIXED ignore list above and never reads this file -- only the newer
# static-asset uploader does. Keeping it costs nothing and it starts working the day this
# project moves to that pipeline; until then it is a statement of intent, not a control.
#
# Two things were tried and measured before `_redirects` was written, so nobody re-tries them:
#   * `_routes.json` cannot do this. It only chooses which requests reach the Function. A
#     request routed to the Function that matches no route of its own falls through to
#     `env.ASSETS.fetch()` and the asset is served anyway -- 200, verified under
#     `wrangler pages dev`. `_routes.json` is still here, pinning the Function to `/api/*`.
#   * `_redirects` rejects a 404 status ("0 valid redirect rules"). 302 to `/` is the form
#     that parses, and a redirect is evaluated before the asset is served.

